JAVA DSA – Day 2
Java Program Execution (JVM, JRE, JDK)
1. Program Execution in Java

Java program execution is the process of converting Java source code into bytecode and executing it using the Java Virtual Machine (JVM).

It involves three main components: Compiler, JVM and JRE.

2. Compiler

A Compiler is a tool that converts human-readable Java source code (.java) into bytecode (.class).

It checks the code for syntax and compilation errors.

It translates the source code into bytecode.

It does not directly convert Java code into machine code.

Java compiler is provided by the JDK.

Example: javac Demo.java generates Demo.class.

3. JVM (Java Virtual Machine)

JVM stands for Java Virtual Machine. It is responsible for executing Java bytecode.

It loads and verifies bytecode before execution.

It uses an interpreter and JIT (Just-In-Time) compiler to execute bytecode.

It converts bytecode into native machine instructions.

It makes Java platform-independent through platform-specific JVM implementations.

4. Bytecode

Bytecode is the intermediate code generated by the Java compiler and stored in a .class file.

It is not directly understandable by the CPU.

It is platform-independent.

It can run on any operating system having a compatible JVM.

Example: The same .class file can run on Windows, Linux and macOS with their respective JVMs.

5. JRE (Java Runtime Environment)

JRE stands for Java Runtime Environment. It provides the environment required to run Java applications.

It includes JVM and supporting runtime libraries.

It provides the resources required for Java program execution.

It is designed for running Java applications, not developing them.

6. JDK (Java Development Kit)

JDK stands for Java Development Kit. It provides the tools required to develop, compile and run Java applications.

It includes JRE components and development tools.

It provides the Java compiler (javac).

It includes tools for debugging and developing Java applications.

